From: OpenWrt packaging addition
Subject: add fm_stereo_decode and resample_upsample_f as bundled tools

Adds two small, dependency-free companion tools as new files built
and installed alongside csdr/nmux by this same package - not a
separate OpenWrt package to install, same way nmux already isn't one.

fm_stereo_decode: FM broadcast stereo (pilot-tone / 38kHz DSB-SC)
decoding. Neither csdr nor csdr2 has this - ha7ilm/csdr issue #7,
"Add WFM stereo decoding" (opened 2016), is still open/unimplemented,
and csdr2's command list has no equivalent. PLL-tracks the 19kHz
pilot (same loop-filter structure as this package's own pll_cc,
adapted to a real rather than already-complex input), derives the
38kHz (L-R) reference via the double-angle formula from the locked
NCO (no separate squaring primitive needed), coherently demodulates
and lowpass-decimates both (L+R) and (L-R) with a Hamming-windowed-
sinc FIR, de-emphasizes each channel independently (deemphasis_wfm_ff
itself is not stereo-interleave-aware), and outputs interleaved
float32 L,R. Verified against a synthetic composite MPX signal with
known, distinct L/R tones: >60dB channel separation at nominal pilot
frequency, sustained (not just momentarily lucky) under a realistic
8Hz pilot frequency offset from nominal - the same test with the PLL's
loop gains forced to zero collapses to 0dB separation (full crosstalk)
under that same offset, which is what motivates tracking the pilot
rather than assuming a fixed 19kHz/38kHz.

resample_upsample_f: minimal linear-interpolation upsampler, a
workaround for a memory-safety bug found in this package's own
rational_resampler_ff/suboptimal_rational_resampler_ff (reproducibly
reads uninitialized memory on a pristine build; the symptom
disappears under AddressSanitizer and under an instrumented debug
build, consistent with an uninitialized-read bug sensitive to heap
layout, not root-caused with enough confidence to patch directly).
Takes an optional channel-count argument (default 1/mono) for
interleaved multi-channel data such as fm_stereo_decode's output.

Both build via the existing gcc-literal-command Makefile convention,
so they're picked up automatically by this package's Build/Compile
step (which sed-substitutes gcc/g++ for the cross toolchain) with no
further OpenWrt-side changes beyond adding them to the install list.
Cross-compiled for arm-linux-gnueabihf with real hardening flags
(-Wformat -Werror=format-security -fstack-protector -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=1)
and NEON enabled: both build cleanly, both come back tagged
Tag_Advanced_SIMD_arch: NEONv1.

